@meta
id: 03-10
title: Drill — two words back
chapter: 03
type: drill
skills: insert-ctrl-w
reps: 8
par: 30
minutes: 5
vscode: select the last two words with the mouse, retype them
open: release-notes.md

@brief
Eight times: the last two words of the line are wrong. Refunds move to the
production fleet, not the staging cluster. Get to the end of the line,
take both words back, type the right ones.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Ctrl-W` | delete the word before the cursor |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
Two presses of `Ctrl-W`, not one. The second one takes the word and the
space in front of it.
--
Press them as a burst — `A` `Ctrl-W` `Ctrl-W` and then type. Do not stop
to check the line between the two deletions.

@solution
    A <Ctrl-W> <Ctrl-W> production fleet <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes; sixteen are the replacement text.

Two `Ctrl-W` presses beat fifteen Backspaces, and more importantly they
beat *counting*. You are not measuring how much to delete, you are saying
"one word, another word" and the editor works out where the boundaries
are. That is the same idea as the word motions in chapter 5, arriving
early and in a smaller form.

If you overshoot, `Ctrl-W` has no undo of its own inside insert mode —
`Esc` then `u` throws away the whole insert, which is usually what you
want anyway.

@start release-notes.md
Refunds move to the staging cluster

@expected release-notes.md
Refunds move to the production fleet
